On Tue, 29 Jan 2019 at 16:14, Zhou Fang <fangzh...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Dear Iñaki,
>
> All types of projects compete simultaneously for machine A and machine B resources. There can be more than 10 types of projects. When there are more resources of machine A available, a new project starts but the new project has a different timeout for machines and uses different number of resources of machines.
>
> For example,
>
> There are 1000 machine A and 1000 machine B available.
> Project 1 uses 100 machine A;
> project 2 uses 200 machine A;
> project 3 uses 500 machine A;
> project 4 uses 400 machine A, so project 4 will wait until project 1 finishes machine A step.
>
> I think it is better to just use one generator. What do you think? Should I use branch? If I use a branch, is the code below correct for the example I mentioned above? Thank you so much!

I don't know. It depends on your arrival process. How do you decide
which project a new arrival will follow? This is what you have to code
in the branch 'option' in case you opt for a single generator.

> env <- simmer("machines")
> env
>
> project <- trajectory("project path") %>%
>

>   branch(1:4, continue = c(T, T, T, T),

This won't work. Instead of 1:4, it should be a function that returns
a number between 1 and 4 based on some decision (see my previous
comment).

>
> trajectory() %>%
>
>   seize("machine A", 100) %>%

>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 15)) %>%

>   release("machine A", 100) %>%
>   seize("machine B", 300) %>%

>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 15)) %>%

>   release("machine B", 300),
>
> trajectory() %>%
>
>   seize("machine A", 200) %>%
>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 40)) %>%
>   release("machine A", 200) %>%
>   seize("machine B", 200) %>%
>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 50)) %>%
>   release("machine B", 200),
>
> trajectory() %>%
>
>   seize("machine A", 500) %>%
>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 20)) %>%
>   release("machine A", 500) %>%
>   seize("machine B", 500) %>%
>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 60)) %>%
>   release("machine B", 500),
>
> trajectory() %>%
>
> seize("machine A", 400) %>%
>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 60)) %>%
>   release("machine A", 400) %>%
> seize("machine B", 500) %>%
>   timeout(function() rnorm(1, 20)) %>%
>   release("machine B", 500))
>
> env %>%
>   add_resource("machine A", 1000) %>%
>   add_resource("machine B", 1000) %>%

>   add_generator("project", project, function() rnorm(1, 10, 2))
>
>
>
>
>
>
> On Tue, Jan 29, 2019 at 3:30 AM Iñaki Ucar <iu...@fedoraproject.org> wrote:
